  "worked_solution": "Computing ${V}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{2} & =V\\cdot V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${V}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $V$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${V}^{7}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{7} & ={V}^{2}\\cdot {V}^{5} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {V}^{5} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{7}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
